  clock: 3  # dbf4
  nonce: dbf4  # dbf4
  message: "DONE — merged as PR #990, dev SHA 3afda2e88b.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED\n  scripts/audit-autopr-convergence — reads .git/AUTOPR_HISTORY.jsonl and prints the (final_state, exit_code) table plus the offending rows. Three-valued verdict: converged / violation / cannot conclude.\n  .agent/hooks/_shared/autopr_convergence_nudge.py — the invocation point, wired into BOTH session start (every mode branch) and the stop hook.\n  29 tests across tests/test_audit_autopr_convergence.py and tests/test_autopr_convergence_nudge.py.\n\nTHE THIRD VERDICT VALUE IS THE DESIGN CENTRE. A missing, empty or partially unparseable ledger yields NO all-clear. This project's recurring defect is a zero meaning \"never modelled\" read as a zero meaning \"nothing wrong\", and an audit that answered \"converged\" over a ledger it could not read would manufacture exactly the reassurance that stopped anyone looking. An unparseable row downgrades the verdict rather than shrinking the denominator, so a corrupting ledger cannot trend toward looking cleaner.\n\nTHE WIRE-UP NEARLY REPRODUCED THIS ITEM, IN THE COMMIT FIXING IT. I wired the nudge into the stop hook first, following the awaits_bakeoff_nudge precedent. stop-hook-preview then reported \"Autonomous mode disabled — stop hook approves immediately\": .agent/hooks/claude-code/stop.sh exits at its OFF branch BEFORE it sources stop_logic.sh, so in OFF mode — the mode this repo runs in — the nudge would never have fired. That is a reader nobody runs, stacked on a ledger nobody read. The primary wire-up is therefore session start, which fires in every mode; test_stop_hook_does_not_run_in_off_mode pins the stop.sh ordering that makes it necessary, so the redundancy gets retired deliberately if that ever changes rather than discovered by accident.\n\nWINDOWED, BECAUSE AN UNREAD INSTRUMENT IS THE FAILURE MODE. The ledger is append-only and never forgets. A nudge over all history would fire forever on a backlog nobody can retire and be tuned out within a week — which is how the ledger got into this state. Default window 25, configurable. It is self-clearing: it speaks about recent misbehaviour and goes quiet as clean runs accumulate.\n\nWHY A SCRIPT AND HOOKS RATHER THAN A CI GATE, stated because it constrains anyone revisiting this: the ledger lives inside .git/, which git never tracks. It is local to a clone, CI can never see it, and a re-clone starts the record over. There is no shared artifact to gate on.\n\nLIVE EVIDENCE, from merged dev. Full history: 483 invocations, VERDICT VIOLATION, 55 runs — reproducing the hand analysis that motivated INV-rahib's post-merge boundary fix, in one second instead of a session. Windowed to 25: 1 violation (PR #963, 2026-09-15, pre-boundary-fix), 20 merged/exit-0. Session start in OFF mode emits the one-line form, verified by sourcing session_start_logic.sh.\n\nONE THING CI CAUGHT THAT I HAD NOT. forge-arms failed with \"assert 25 == 9\": that job installs pytest and nothing else on purpose, so PyYAML is absent and load_window takes its documented missing-yaml fallback. My test had assumed yaml was importable, making it a claim about the runner's dependency set rather than about the function. The sibling awaits_bakeoff_nudge.py has the identical dependency and never tripped it, purely because forge-arms selects tests/test_autopr_*.py and its file is named test_awaits_bakeoff_nudge.py — the glob differed, not the dependency. Fixed by asserting both documented arms rather than a skipif, and verified in a venv built the way forge-arms builds one (302 passed with yaml confirmed absent).\n\nRESIDUAL: the audit reports; it does not fix. The 55 violations it reports are INV-rahib (class 1, fixed forward by PR #987) and WI-nazoj (class 2, open). This item was about the reader, and the reader exists and is read."  # dbf4
